  "question": {
    "text": "If \\(\\alpha, \\beta\\) are the roots of the equation \\(\\mathrm{ax}^2+\\mathrm{bx}+\\mathrm{c}=0\\), then the roots of the equation \\(\\mathrm{ax}^2+\\mathrm{bx}(\\mathrm{x}+1)+\\mathrm{c}(\\mathrm{x}+1)^2=0\\) are",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "WtME2",
      "text": "\\(\\alpha-1, \\beta-1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CEK2f",
      "text": "\\(\\alpha+1, \\beta+1\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "YZo8l",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{\\alpha}{\\alpha-1}, \\frac{\\beta}{\\beta-1}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "WgPRm",
      "text": "\\(\\frac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}, \\frac{\\beta}{1-\\beta}\\)",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The second equation can be rewritten as\n\\(a\\left(\\frac{x}{x+1}\\right)^2+b\\left(\\frac{x}{x+1}\\right)+c=0\\)\nand hence its roots correspond to \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{\\mathrm{x}+1}=\\alpha\\) and \\(\\frac{x}{x+1}=\\beta\\).\nHence \\(x=\\frac{\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\) and \\(\\frac{\\beta}{1-\\beta}\\).",
    "image": null
  },
